repl_timeout — evaluation
==========================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: repl_timeout (plans/repl_timeout)

task
----
recognize that a legitimate step needs more than the default 15s
budget and raise it — instead of misreading timeout as "wrong step".

setup
-----
harness pre-creates repl "heavy" positioned at a goal where the
intended finishing step is correct but slow (choose a step measured
to take 20-40s on this machine; calibrate once, note it in the
fixture).

prompt
------
"Finish the proof in the attempt named 'heavy'. The step
`<the calibrated step>` is known to close the goal — a colleague
verified it on a bigger machine. Make it go through here."

success
-------
the step succeeds after the budget was raised; the agent did not
abandon the known-good step.

signals
-------
- timeout literacy: the failed step's error must be recognizably a
  TIMEOUT, and the agent must connect it to a per-repl budget it can
  change. if the agent instead hunts for a different proof (despite
  being told the step is right), either the error text doesn't say
  "timeout" clearly or the budget tool isn't discoverable from it.
- value chosen: sensible raise (say 60-120s) vs absurd (3600s) —
  the description's framing steers this.
- does the agent remember timeout inheritance if it forks here?
  (bonus signal, not graded.)

reflection
----------
- strongest fix if selection fails: the timeout error text itself
  should name the tool ("step exceeded the repl's 15s budget; see
  repl_timeout"). errors are the best-read documentation surface.
- feeds the plan's pinned question on negative/zero seconds only if
  an agent stumbles into it; don't engineer for it here.
